<EnterpriseDetail
  slug="scale"
  seoTitle="Scale and Scheduling | SAM Enterprise"
  seoDescription="How SAM places many agent sessions on cloud machines: capacity pools across providers, reservation-based packing, warm reuse and automatic cleanup."
  title="Many agents, few idle machines"
  subtitle="Start work from chat, a schedule, a GitHub event or a webhook. SAM finds a machine with room or creates one, and deletes machines nobody is using."
>
  <h2>Capacity pools</h2>
  <p>
    A capacity pool lists the cloud accounts, providers, regions and machine types SAM may use, with a strategy
    for choosing between them and a limit on how many machines it can run. Pools can be set for the whole
    installation, a user or a project, and the most specific one applies. When a provider runs out of capacity,
    the pool's exhaustion policy decides whether to wait and retry, try the pool's other allowed machines, or
    stop with an error.
  </p>
  <ShotFrame
    src="/images/features/sam-compute-pool-editor.png"
    alt="The capacity pool editor in SAM, with its sources, allowed machine offerings, placement strategy and limits."
    caption="A capacity pool: which accounts and machine types SAM may use, and how it chooses."
  />

  <h2>Packing work onto machines</h2>
  <p>
    Each task or chat declares how much CPU and memory it needs, or inherits a default from its profile. SAM
    places it on one of the user's machines with that much unreserved capacity, and only creates a new machine
    when none has room. Every placement decision is recorded, so you can see why work landed where it did.
  </p>

  <h2>Cleanup</h2>
  <p>
    An empty machine stays warm for 30 minutes by default for the next task, then SAM deletes it. Durable Object
    alarms, a scheduled sweep and a maximum lifetime for automatically created machines all catch machines that
    a missed signal would otherwise leave running.
  </p>

  <h2>How far it goes</h2>
  <p>
    The practical limit is your cloud accounts' quotas. Hetzner, for example, caps how many servers an account
    can run, and SAM reads provider limits so it can stop placing work before a request fails. Spreading a pool
    across several providers and regions raises the ceiling.
  </p>

  <h2>Typical uses</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Migrations</strong>: one session per service or module, each in its own workspace and branch.</li>
    <li><strong>Audits</strong>: the same review prompt run across many repositories.</li>
    <li><strong>Scheduled maintenance</strong>: dependency updates or lint fixes from a schedule.</li>
    <li><strong>Event-driven triage</strong>: an agent for every new issue with a given label.</li>
  </ul>
</EnterpriseDetail>
